  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "How many times must a regular die be rolled to be at least 90% certain that you will obtain at least one six?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p9-q12-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "13 rolls.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p9-q12-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use the complement: 13 is the smallest integer ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "n",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " for which ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n\\le\\frac1{10}",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ".",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $A$ be the event of obtaining at least one six in $n$ rolls. Its complement is obtaining no sixes. Each roll has probability $\\frac56$ of not showing a six, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(A')&=\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n\\\\\\Pr(A)&=1-\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n.\\end{align*}$$\nWe require\n$$\\begin{align*}1-\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n&\\ge\\frac9{10}\\\\\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n&\\le\\frac1{10}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor 12 rolls, $\\left(\\frac56\\right)^{12}>\\frac1{10}$, so 12 rolls are insufficient. For 13 rolls, $\\left(\\frac56\\right)^{13}<\\frac1{10}$, so 13 rolls are sufficient. Since $\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n$ decreases as $n$ increases, the minimum is **13 rolls**.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
